  "body": "## What changed\n\n`crates/app/tests/fixture_library.rs`, test `the_library_folder_is_the_override_else_a_sibling_of_machines` only. No product code.\n\nThe test fed Unix-only paths (`/shop/fixtures`, `/Users/me`, `/home/me`). `fixture_library::user_dir_for` (`store.rs`) keeps an override only when `PathBuf::is_absolute()` is true on the host, so on a Windows host those paths were dropped and the assertions failed.\n\n- The override path, the macOS HOME and the linux HOME are now picked per OS with `cfg!(windows)` (`C:\\shop\\fixtures`, `C:\\Users\\me`, `C:\\home\\me` on Windows; the old strings elsewhere), the same pattern as `machine_edit.rs` `the_user_folder_resolves_from_the_override_else_the_platform_data_folder`.\n- Expected folders are built with `PathBuf::from(home).join(...)`, so separators are right on both hosts.\n- The `env` helper now copies its pairs into owned strings instead of requiring `&'static` literals, so runtime-chosen per-OS strings can be passed.\n- Assertions keep their meaning: an absolute override wins; a relative override (`\"relative\"`) falls back to HOME; the macOS and linux data folders are unchanged; `linux` with no HOME gives `None`. `library_path(&PathBuf::from(\"/x\"))` is unchanged: it only checks the file name suffix, which holds on any host.\n\n## Why it passes on Windows\n\nOn Windows the three host-absolute paths are absolute, so `CAMCRAFT_FIXTURES` is kept and the HOME branches resolve under `C:\\Users\\me` / `C:\\home\\me`. The relative override stays non-absolute on Windows too, so it falls back to HOME. The expected `PathBuf`s are built from the same strings with `join`, so they compare equal to what `user_dir_for` produces. This is the same construction as `machine_edit.rs`, which already passes in the Windows batch.\n\n## How tested\n\n- macOS: `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test fixture_library -- the_library_folder_is_the_override_else_a_sibling_of_machines --exact` - 1 passed.\n- macOS: `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --test fixture_library -- -D warnings` - clean.\n- Linux quick gate against upstream main: `app fixture_library (1 fn)` ok.\n- **Windows 11 on ARM (aarch64-pc-windows-msvc), before and after:**\n  - NeoTech main @ 5fd45923: `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test fixture_library` - **5 passed, 1 failed**: `the_library_folder_is_the_override_else_a_sibling_of_machines` panics at `fixture_library.rs:117:5`, `left: None, right: Some(\"/shop/fixtures\")` - the failure #723 reports.\n  - This branch @ ae39b16f: the same command - **6 passed, 0 failed**.\n- Pinning test: `fixture_library::the_library_folder_is_the_override_else_a_sibling_of_machines`.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- Windows on x86-64 (the maintainer's batch host): not run here; the path rule the test exercises is the same on ARM64 and x86-64 Windows.\n- The other `fixture_library` tests and the full suite were not run in this PR (the quick gate ran the changed test binary only).\n\nChangelog: none\n\nFork synced at v0.16.0.\n\nFixes #723\n\n",
